Anal Fissure
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Severe tearing pain during defecation, Prolonged post-defecatory pain, Bright red blood on toilet paper, Visible posterior midline mucosal tear

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-003
Reason for Visit: Unbearable pain during and after bowel movements with fresh rectal bleeding, resulting in extreme distress and functional impairment.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient only.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage confirmed extreme, persistent perianal pain and fresh bleeding, causing severe distress and deliberate retention of feces. Urgent assessment was required to rule out an acutely thrombosed hemorrhoid or perianal abscess, provide immediate topical analgesia, and prevent fecal impaction prior to the next standard consulting period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "Passing a stool feels like I'm passing broken glass, and the throbbing pain lasts for hours afterwards."
History of Present Illness: Reports severe, sharp, 'tearing' pain during defecation that started 3 days ago following an episode of constipation and passing a large, hard stool. The pain evolves into an intense burning and throbbing sensation lasting for 2-3 hours post-defecation. Notes small amounts of bright red blood on the toilet paper. Expresses extreme reluctance to defecate due to fear of pain.
Functional Status: Ambulating cautiously; unable to sit directly on buttocks.
Social/Safety Concerns: Risk of severe fecal impaction due to stool withholding behaviors.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/90 (Pain mediated) | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient appears highly anxious, shifting weight constantly while standing.
Physical Exam: Gentle separation of the buttocks reveals a visible linear mucosal tear in the anoderm at the posterior midline. Severe spasm of the internal anal sphincter noted. Digital rectal examination deferred due to extreme pain and visible pathology. Findings match the diagnostic tetrad of severe tearing pain during defecation, prolonged post-defecatory pain, bright red blood on toilet paper, and visible posterior midline mucosal tear.
Environmental Observations: Over-the-counter codeine-based pain relief observed on counter, which likely exacerbated initial constipation.
Medication Reconciliation: Discarded codeine. taking paracetamol with minimal effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Anal Fissure - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Thrombosed External Hemorrhoid - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of a palpable, tense, exquisitely tender, bluish subcutaneous lump at the anal verge.

Perianal Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fluctuant, erythematous perianal mass and normal body temperature.

Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn's) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Solitary posterior midline location rather than multiple, eccentric, or painless fissures, and absence of chronic diarrhea.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's presentation of agonizing, tearing post-defecatory pain and fear of bowel movements necessitated prompt medical intervention. The acute functional impairment and high distress levels warranted an urgent after-hours visit to physically examine the perineum, exclude acute sepsis or thrombosed hemorrhoid, and prescribe targeted sphincter-relaxing therapies (topical nitrates/calcium channel blockers) and appropriate osmotic laxatives. Deferring care would have resulted in worsening severe pain, acute urinary retention (sympathetic response), or secondary fecal impaction.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed topical Rectogesic (Glyceryl Trinitrate 0.2% ointment) applied BD, and Macrogol 3350 (Movicol) 1-2 sachets daily.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply the prescribed ointment 1 cm inside the anal verge immediately prior to a bowel movement. This directly relaxes the anal sphincter muscle, reducing the tearing sensation and allowing the fissure to heal without repeated trauma.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Sitz bath basin recommended.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks to assess healing and titrate laxatives.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Seek prompt GP review or present to an Urgent Care Centre if pain becomes completely intractable, if a painful lump develops near the anus, or if a fever >38.0°C occurs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Most fissures heal with conservative management, but chronic fissures may require surgical referral.
Clinical Course: Pain should reduce significantly within 48-72 hours of starting the ointment and laxatives. Full healing takes 4-6 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you develop a high fever, severe constant perineal pain not related to bowel movements, or difficulty passing urine, seek emergency medical care.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The fissure is a tear kept open by the spasm of your anal muscle. The ointment relaxes this muscle, reducing pain and increasing blood flow to heal the tear. The laxative ensures the stool is soft and prevents further tearing.
Actionable Steps: Soak in a warm sitz bath for 10-15 minutes after each bowel movement. The heat physically relaxes the spasming muscle and cleanses the area, which significantly reduces the hours of throbbing pain you currently experience. Stop using codeine immediately.

